rise of the Tomb Raider unfolds in a  valley haunted by echoes of older times  abandoned structures dot the landscape  while bygone kingdoms lie dormant  beneath the ground even the current  inhabitants long sheltered from the  outside world still hunt and gather as  their ancestors did and for Lara Croft a  person intent on escaping her past this  is not a comforting place but in her  pursuit of supernatural artifacts go he  or she must so begins the next adventure  for the iconic Explorer  in 2013's Tomb Raider reboot developer  Crystal Dynamics thrust Laura into  danger against her will  ruining her on a strange Island with  even stranger secrets in rise of the  Tomb Raider she's running headlong into  the fray at her own pace seeking the key  to eternal life if that premise sounds  trite that's understandable  the Holy Grail grants the same power in  Indiana Jones and the Last Crusade and  Nathan Drake a more apt and recent  counterpart spent uncharted 3 hunting  for the Fountain of Youth  yet rise of the Tomb Raider anchors  Lara's story with its own believable  characters and emotional weight  Lara refuses to acknowledge her father's  death opting instead to seek the divine  source the object that could stave off  death and grief for good Crystal  Dynamics doesn't use magic as a  storytelling crutch though it injects  mystical elements in a way that makes  sense within the world they occupy and  on the other hand as a third-person  adventure game  rise of the Tomb Raider excels rather  than collide gameplay and stories share  the same space supplementing each other  and emerging as a cohesive whole like  its predecessor rise of the Tomb Raider  places you in an expansive world filled  with soldiers of the shady Trinity  organization  this isn't a sandbox game though  subsequent areas are gated by ability  unlocks you gain certain items to reach  new self contains owns in the story but  can also return to older areas with  newfound abilities by navigating three  separate skill trees you can build Lara  however you want as long as it fits your  playstyle the resulting backtracking is  rewarding - it highlights just how much  Lara has improved in the meantime  a Byzantine war girl  there are also more actual tombs this  time around these catacombs are also  well worth your time both are the new  abilities they unlock and the design of  the platforming puzzles inside you alter  water levels operate pulleys and shatter  icy barriers all the while working  toward improvements to Lara's climbing  hunting and fighting skills they're the  kind of puzzles that make you feel smart  as if you somehow forwarded Crystal  Dynamics in the process  codex and in your fight against enemy  soldiers and nature itself you also  gather resources for healing salves and  special ammunition when stranded in the  wilderness with few supplies and little  on her back laura relies on nature for  survival unfortunately this process  becomes tedious after only a short time  it devolves into a constant process of  pressing the same button on identical  trees and bushes and dead rabbits over  and over again rise of the tomb Raider's  world is stunning but I was often too  distracted by the repetitive abundance  of resources on an otherwise beautiful  canvas but gathering is part of the  overall improvement process and it makes  sense in this harsh landscape Lara treks  through and this is where I rise the  Tomb Raider truly shines Crystal  Dynamics erases the barrier between  gameplay and story making an almost  seamless experience in the process  exploration leads to gathering which  leads to crafting which leads to combat  which leads to new locations and  throughout the game cutscenes are  succinct but not without substance  they don't believe her the narrative  monologues and grandiose asides but  still manage to build character and  establish tone there's a surprising  amount of detail here - Lara warms her  hands over fires shiver when the wind  picks up and slides her feet through  taller piles of snow there's enough  exposition here to provide meaningful  context but not enough to get in the way  of well having fun  and when it comes to combat rise of the  Tomb Raider is superb the variety of  options available in any given firefight  is staggering in fact these encounters  often feel more like puzzles I opted for  stealth when I could waiting for a lone  soldier to stray from the pack before  finishing him or sending arrows into  snipers from a distance environmental  objects aided me as well as Lara's  crafting abilities let her fashion  molotovs and cans of shrapnel at a  moment's notice it's just another way  rise of the Tomb Raider paints Lara as a  survivor she's resourceful there are  slight difficulty spikes late in the  game but if you've explored enough and  spent your hard-earned researches on new  tools the fight will be challenging but  not overly so  rise of the tomb Raider's first shot  pans over a vast foreboding landscape  will soon come to know on an intimate  level in many ways this functions as a  promise on the part of Crystal Dynamics  that there are big things ahead of us  and at the end of Lara's journey after  we've seen her through this adventure  and experience everything the world has  to offer it's clear that that promise  was kept
 

